sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Where a cylindrical cell of uniform wall tension is capped by part of a sphere, the sphere's radius is twice the cylinder's radius, the cap subtends a cone of 60°, and its height is about 0.27 of the cylinder radius (a little more than one-eighth of the diameter), proportions seen in the free end cells of Spirogyra.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A cylindrical cell's end cap has twice the cylinder's radius and height about 0.27R";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "R_{cap} = 2R; cap subtends a cone of 60^\\circ; cap height = R(2 - \\sqrt{3}) \\approx 0.27R";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Spherical caps on cylindrical cells”. Scope: cylindrical cells with free spherical ends, e.g. Spirogyra and other filamentous algae"
}
